  "body": "https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/parsing.html#hexademical-character-reference-start-state\r\n\r\n> 12\\.2\\.5\\.76 Hexademical character reference start state\r\n> \r\n> Consume the next input character:​\r\n> \r\n> ASCII hex digit\r\n> Reconsume in the hexademical character reference state\\.\r\n> Anything else\r\n> This is an absence\\-of\\-digits\\-in\\-numeric\\-character\\-reference parse error\\. Flush code points consumed as a character reference\\. Reconsume in the return state\\.\r\n\r\n\"ASCII hex digit\" is defined in Infra as:\r\n\r\n> An ASCII hex digit is an **ASCII upper hex digit** or **ASCII lower hex digit**.\r\n\r\nOnce in the `hexadecimal character reference state`, the check is for either \"ASCII hex digit\" or \"ASCII digit\", so the distinction is made. That seems to mean that a hex character reference’s first digit cannot be 0–9, unless I’m missing something, and that doesn’t seem to be true in practice (nor would it make much sense).",
